<BaseLayout title="About — Mishti Broor">
  <section class="page-hero"><div class="container"><div class="eyebrow">About</div><h1>Biomedical engineer building toward bioelectronics and neural sensing.</h1><p>I’m pursuing an M.S. in Biomedical Engineering at Northwestern University after completing my B.S. in BME at UIC. My work spans EEG hardware, biosignal analysis, rapid prototyping, human-subject studies, and technical communication.</p></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="container prose">
    <h2>Background</h2>
    <p>My undergraduate research centered on developing EEG hardware for unbraided and cornrowed hair. That project took me from CAD and resin 3D printing into multimodal human-subject experiments, high-density EEG acquisition, impedance optimization, reproducible signal analysis, and manuscript preparation.</p>
    <p>I’m now continuing into clinical EEG at Northwestern while intentionally expanding toward bioelectronics, wearable sensing, embedded hardware, and device translation.</p>

    <h2>Technical focus</h2>
    <p><strong>Hardware & prototyping:</strong> CAD modeling, SolidWorks, Fusion 360, Blender, resin/MSLA 3D printing, rapid prototyping, Arduino.</p>
    <p><strong>EEG & signal processing:</strong> BrainVision, MATLAB, Python, MNE-Python, digital filtering, spectral analysis, CSP, scikit-learn.</p>
    <p><strong>Human studies:</strong> EEG data acquisition, IMUs, force-sensor insoles, impedance optimization, IRB-guided research workflows.</p>
    <p><strong>Currently developing:</strong> analog front ends, PCB design, embedded systems, and wearable bioinstrumentation.</p>

    <h2>Outside the lab</h2>
    <p>I write about emerging neurotechnology and wearable health technologies, particularly how technical ideas move from research prototypes into products people can actually use.</p>

